The reason is, you
see here, specifically

00:36:36.710 --> 00:36:38.030
heat is a constant.

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:43.360
That means the energy
is T, proportional to T.

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:48.010
And so when you get
to higher temperature,

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:49.360
photon has higher energy.

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:54.330
They move more violently
and they scatter more.

00:36:54.330 --> 00:36:59.130
So in this regime,
the relaxation time

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:01.720
is the inverse
proportionate to energy,

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:05.700
so that's the inverse
proportional to temperature.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:10.970
Once they go through this
regime, as you cool down,

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:12.980
the photon scatter and weakens.

00:37:12.980 --> 00:37:16.670
So photon scattering
do not happen

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:19.190
as you go to lower temperature.

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:21.770
The mean free path becomes long.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:24.545
And typically, in this
regime, the mean free path

00:37:24.545 --> 00:37:26.450
can get to centimeters.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:30.360
So that reached the sample
size, and the mean free path

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:33.960
becomes comparable
to the sample size.

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:36.010
So it's a constant.

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:39.510
And then the mean free path
is tau times v, is a constant.

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:45.690
Specific heat is appropriate
to cubic, thermal conductivity

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:46.800
is appropriate to cubic.

00:37:49.490 --> 00:37:55.010
And in this regime, where
our fallen photon is weak,

00:37:55.010 --> 00:37:58.720
what's really important
is impurities.

00:37:58.720 --> 00:38:01.180
I mentioned they say
impurity scatter,

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:04.430
like really scatters--
fourth power.

00:38:04.430 --> 00:38:12.250
And so for example, diamond,
which is the best conductor,

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:15.580
it turns out that most
material, it has a isotopes--

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:17.750
diamond, silicon.

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:20.120
If you make them--

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:26.600
even remove the isotope, just
to make the same atomic mass,

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:30.140
isotopically enrich
it, you actually

00:38:30.140 --> 00:38:34.350
boost the thermal
conductivity by a few times

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.640
if you can do that.

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:38.590
So you want at the
room temperature there.

00:38:38.590 --> 00:38:42.310
I don't know whether that's
settled, but most likely,

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:44.140
even at room temperature
in most material

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:50.620
like silicon and diamond, the
isotope, if you purify it,

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:52.450
the isotope will
actually-- you can

00:38:52.450 --> 00:38:53.830
increase thermal conductivity.

00:38:53.830 --> 00:38:56.150
So I heard this
a few years back,

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:58.840
probably 15 years back,
they were reported

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:01.930
that isotope-enriched
silicon has a 60% higher

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:03.960
thermal conductivity.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:07.770
And somebody told me there was
a big factory built just to make

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:10.920
the isothermal rearrange the
silicon because the heat cannot

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:13.320
go out from the chips.

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:16.890
I'm not sure whether they were
successful at the end or not.

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:23.110
So that's thermal conductivity.

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:27.260
This is the example
of Fourier's law.

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:32.080
Let's look at the flow problem.

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:47.020
OK.

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:50.470
Now I'm going to look at
the Newton Shear Stress.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:53.170
I have a flow.

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:56.230
And the simplest flow
is a [INAUDIBLE] flow.

00:39:56.230 --> 00:39:58.330
When I have one plate
fixed, the other plate

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:01.570
drag parallel plates.

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:07.310
And the velocity-- say if this
is my x, so my dl was this.

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:09.010
This is my y.

00:40:09.010 --> 00:40:13.930
And I have-- in this
case, when I have flow,

00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:17.410
a significant difference
is now the average velocity

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:19.600
of the molecule is not zero.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:27.550
So it's not completely random.

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:33.740
And in the [INAUDIBLE] flow, I
have linear velocity profile.

00:40:33.740 --> 00:40:39.050
So any place I have velocity,
u, and the v, of course,

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:41.500
is 0 for the [INAUDIBLE] flow.

00:40:41.500 --> 00:40:44.650
And v u is the function y.

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:57.260
So in this case, the f0,
what's the proper f0?

00:40:57.260 --> 00:41:02.360
And you can go to saw really
the full words of my equation.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:08.690
But a good approximation this
is a part of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:11.660
equation is the f0 now is--

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:14.600
rather than f0 is an fd.

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:17.900
it's a displaced Boltzmann.

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:22.430
And the displaced Boltzmann
is say-- remember,

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:25.430
the Boltzmann factor
is mv squared,

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:29.450
divided by exponential
energy, divided by kT, right?

00:41:29.450 --> 00:41:37.040
So energy is one
half mv squared, kbT.

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:42.170
And mv, v is now
the random velocity,

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:46.280
minus the average
velocity squared.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:51.890
It doesn't matter.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:56.720
So that's a displaced.

00:41:56.720 --> 00:42:00.080
So v is the random velocity.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:04.970
And [INAUDIBLE] the
phase space velocity.

00:42:04.970 --> 00:42:08.740
And if you average v, you
get a average velocity, u.

00:42:08.740 --> 00:42:11.320
So you subtract
that amount, this

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:15.430
is the Boltzmann distribute,
displaced Boltzmann.

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:20.290
And then I have n, the
density, in the front.

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:31.000
And I forgot whether the m is
the denominator or numerator.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:41.260
So m, 2 pi, kbT, 3
[? Hoffs ?] and exponential.

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:48.880
So now I'm going to replace
in my relaxation time

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:54.390
approximation, replace f0 by fd.

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:58.830
And this, of course, it
means in my current problem,

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:04.590
when I have a laminar flow, long
zero velocity is the only u.

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:06.760
I want to make my life easier.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:10.950
So what I have is a
vx minus u squared,

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:15.840
plus vy squared,
plus vz squared.

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:17.958
And divide by 2 kbT.

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:22.050
The rest is the same.

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:28.020
So this is my fd.

00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:32.040
And in this case, I'm
going to replace f0

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:34.740
by fd, displaced Boltzmann.

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:37.050
So this is f0, fd.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.360
This is dfd.

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:43.510
Now I have to do
that derivative.

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:46.420
Go back to my general solution.

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:51.480
Again, say I don't have a
force acting on the molecule--

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:55.090
as long as I forget about
gravitational force.

00:43:55.090 --> 00:43:58.465
Pressure is not a force in
my picture, it's a momentum.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.820
So I drop this.

00:44:04.820 --> 00:44:10.160
I have v, df, d, d, r--

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:16.840
so r, remember in here,
this u is a function of y.

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:21.070
vx, vy, vz, are again,
my phase based variable.

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:25.580
They are independent of xyz.

00:44:30.440 --> 00:44:32.840
So I'm going to
sum up vx, vy, vz.

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:35.070
My real term is here.

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:41.120
So if I look at my f is there.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:44.550
fd, minus tau.

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:51.840
And if I look at this, v
[INAUDIBLE],, creating the rfd.

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.270
What I have here?

00:44:54.270 --> 00:45:00.445
I have-- because when I do this
gradient with respect to r,

00:45:00.445 --> 00:45:05.300
I have dx, which cancels,
because nothing depends on x.

00:45:05.300 --> 00:45:08.450
I have dy, because u
is a function of y.

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:14.330
u is a function of y.

00:45:14.330 --> 00:45:16.530
So I have the y term.

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:21.290
So I should keep vy,
because it's v dot gradient.

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:30.050
So what I have here
is tau vy and df 0 du.

00:45:30.050 --> 00:45:35.450
And times du, dy.

00:45:35.450 --> 00:45:43.790
This is my solution for the
Boltzmann equation casted

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:48.730
under the current problem here.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:50.710
So I have f.

00:45:50.710 --> 00:45:55.000
My [INAUDIBLE] is going to
calculate the shear stress,

00:45:55.000 --> 00:46:00.190
in terms of the
distribution function, f,

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:03.620
and the random momentum
space variables.

00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:06.550
So what's my shear stress?

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:22.650
So when I consider
what's a shear stress,

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:25.620
I have velocity flow, vx.

00:46:25.620 --> 00:46:28.710
The molecule is
doing random motion.

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:30.270
I have vx, vy.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:38.370
So the shear stress is due to--

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:40.620
so in the tau xy.

00:46:40.620 --> 00:46:42.990
What's my tau xy?

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:44.010
What's the shear stress?

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:48.390
It's due to the momentum
exchange in the x direction

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:51.410
going across the plane y.

00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:56.390
The molecule go
carry momentum, mvx.

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:59.210
And because the component,
some of this molecule

00:46:59.210 --> 00:47:03.670
going down, sometimes going
up in the y direction.

00:47:03.670 --> 00:47:05.650
So it's that
exchange of momentum

00:47:05.650 --> 00:47:10.790
in the y direction across
this plane of the x momentum

00:47:10.790 --> 00:47:12.545
component gives me
the shear stress.

00:47:15.340 --> 00:47:22.380
So when I do the
shear, tau xy, and I

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:24.420
will have to integrate now.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:26.430
I'm not doing
[INAUDIBLE] because I'm

00:47:26.430 --> 00:47:29.100
using Maxwell
distribution-- vx, vy,

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:32.710
vz are treated as a
continuous variable.

00:47:32.710 --> 00:47:40.190
So I have minus infinite to
plus infinite integration limit.

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:47.330
And my shear stress is mvx,
That's movement in x direction.

00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:49.560
The rate of change
in the y direction,

00:47:49.560 --> 00:47:52.850
that's the molecule
going across the plane.

00:47:55.690 --> 00:47:58.000
That will give you
the molecular level

00:47:58.000 --> 00:47:59.830
expression for shear stress.

00:47:59.830 --> 00:48:04.210
And the f, dvx, dvy, dvz.

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:19.230
So now you substitute
your f we have here.

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:22.370
And you do an integration.

00:48:22.370 --> 00:48:26.430
And this is a good example
where your question again,

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:30.560
is do I convert it into
a spherical coordinate?

00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:33.050
Do I keep the original
[INAUDIBLE],, vx, vy,

00:48:33.050 --> 00:48:35.120
vz coordinate?

00:48:35.120 --> 00:48:39.480
Or you want to convert it
into v, the energy, right--

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:41.670
mv squared.

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:44.790
And for this example, it's
much easier to actually keep

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:48.080
separate-- vx, vy, vz--

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:49.470
and do your integration.

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:51.890
So this is where
I say, once you're

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:55.610
comfortable starting
point, writing this down.

00:48:55.610 --> 00:48:59.150
And the rest is your
own [INAUDIBLE] choice,

00:48:59.150 --> 00:49:02.960
which you do.

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:09.110
In fact, if you
jump a few steps,

00:49:09.110 --> 00:49:11.030
this term always drop out.

00:49:11.030 --> 00:49:12.980
The equilibrium term
always drop out.

00:49:15.590 --> 00:49:19.090
And you do your math,
it's due to symmetry.

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:20.320
It's an odd function.

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:24.060
When you integrate from minus
infinite to plus infinite,

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:26.450
it cancels out.

00:49:26.450 --> 00:49:30.460
Physically, what it means is
because the f is isotropic--

00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:34.670
in each direction is the same--

00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:47.220
so if you look at the
solution f0, and f0--

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:51.660
f is an f0 solution,
superimposed with a deviation

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:53.530
term.

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:57.510
So deviation term, in the
case of heat conduction,

00:49:57.510 --> 00:50:00.390
in the forward
direction is a positive,

00:50:00.390 --> 00:50:03.640
in the backward
direction is a negative.

00:50:03.640 --> 00:50:08.490
So it's actually this imbalance,
this is your g we drew before.

00:50:08.490 --> 00:50:12.820
That's your g, the difference
between f and the f0.

00:50:12.820 --> 00:50:17.960
It's this imbalance that's
giving the left flux.

00:50:17.960 --> 00:50:19.910
f0 itself is the equivalent.

00:50:19.910 --> 00:50:23.240
They're equal going
forward, going backward,

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:25.520
so there's no left flux.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:29.120
Now when I have transport this
one in the forward direction

00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:33.380
more, in the backward direction
less, so the imbalance give me

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:37.600
the heat flux, and give me
momentum flux-- all this.

00:50:37.600 --> 00:50:44.870
That's the physics behind
this mathematical expression.

00:50:44.870 --> 00:50:48.790
So if I substitute this
in, and what you find out

00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:58.270
is I write it m 2 pi kbT, 3/2.

00:50:58.270 --> 00:51:01.510
And I'm going to
pull this tau out.

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:05.870
I'm going to do a
constant relaxation time.

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:08.030
And relax time, there are
different model again,

00:51:08.030 --> 00:51:09.950
for relaxation time.

00:51:09.950 --> 00:51:15.560
And minus infinite
to plus infinite--

00:51:15.560 --> 00:51:26.060
and it turns out, exponential
mvz squared, 2 kbT.

00:51:26.060 --> 00:51:31.040
And dvz-- so I'm doing
each term separately.

00:51:31.040 --> 00:51:36.020
And I have my other
two terms and I

00:51:36.020 --> 00:51:40.460
want to write this
out just to emphasize,

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:42.950
when you do a
different problem, you

00:51:42.950 --> 00:51:45.260
may have to choose your
different coordinate.

00:51:45.260 --> 00:51:47.210
And that's your freedom.

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:50.510
vy squared, 2 kbT--

00:51:50.510 --> 00:51:53.780
so this is the dvy.

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:58.520
And then vz, minus
infinite to plus infinite.

00:51:58.520 --> 00:52:03.200
And mvx, minus u.

00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:06.530
And this is a kbT.

00:52:06.530 --> 00:52:10.670
That's what it comes around
for df0 du, because the df0,

00:52:10.670 --> 00:52:13.970
du have this u in the exponent.

00:52:13.970 --> 00:52:16.970
That's what I have here.

00:52:16.970 --> 00:52:28.940
And exponential minus mvx,
minus u squared, and 2 kbT.

00:52:31.460 --> 00:52:33.230
So dvx.

00:52:37.360 --> 00:52:42.100
And then also, I have
in the front eventually

00:52:42.100 --> 00:52:44.980
I have the du, dy.

00:52:44.980 --> 00:52:47.440
That's the Newton
shear stress law.

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:50.770
I pull it out, because nothing--

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:52.970
this is a real space quantity.

00:52:52.970 --> 00:52:55.855
It's not integrated
over the momentum space.

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:00.960
So you can integrate each one.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:03.930
You can look at the
integration table

00:53:03.930 --> 00:53:07.280
if you don't know how to
do those integrations.

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:11.460
And you'll find out at the
end, those are all constants.

00:53:11.460 --> 00:53:16.960
So what I have is really
viscosity expression.

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:25.530
So what I'm going to get
out of this, all of this--

00:53:39.397 --> 00:53:49.170
This one I'll have
n tau, kbT, du, dy.

00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:52.920
This is my Newton shear
stress [INAUDIBLE] du, dy.

00:53:52.920 --> 00:54:00.330
So I have now expression for
viscosity of the molecules

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:03.750
is n tau kbT.

00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:14.330
You can also do the
thermal conductivity.

00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:15.610
This is the momentum.

00:54:15.610 --> 00:54:18.610
You do your energy, you
get thermal conductivity.

00:54:18.610 --> 00:54:23.500
So thermal conductivity,
k, for gas molecule,

00:54:23.500 --> 00:54:25.360
is you do your mass.

00:54:25.360 --> 00:54:33.910
You'll get the 2/5 kbm, kbT.

00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:38.560
so it turns out
[INAUDIBLE] times n tau.

00:54:38.560 --> 00:54:45.430
So it's relate to
viscosity kbm mu.

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:50.220
So thermal conductivity is
related to the viscosity,

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:51.270
[INAUDIBLE] molecules.

00:54:56.820 --> 00:54:59.220
and there are a lot
of this relation,

00:54:59.220 --> 00:55:03.060
for example, in electricity
we're getting in soon.

00:55:03.060 --> 00:55:05.250
The electrical
activity is related

00:55:05.250 --> 00:55:06.900
to thermal conductivity.

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:10.530
That's all because there's
just a different integrals

00:55:10.530 --> 00:55:12.710
of the Boltzmann equation.

00:55:12.710 --> 00:55:15.080
I mean, this integral, what
you have here, for example,

00:55:15.080 --> 00:55:17.450
is relaxation time.

00:55:17.450 --> 00:55:19.560
And a different
molecular expression,

00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:23.910
whether it's a shear
stress, vy times mvx,

00:55:23.910 --> 00:55:26.870
or it's energy of
1/2 mv squared.

00:55:26.870 --> 00:55:30.370
That's you're doing
the integration

00:55:30.370 --> 00:55:34.660
of different functions,
different components

00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:37.840
carried by the same molecule.

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:42.330
Any questions?

00:55:44.685 --> 00:55:45.560
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:45.560 --> 00:55:46.530
GANG CHEN: Yes.

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:48.620
AUDIENCE: One thing
usually we say

00:55:48.620 --> 00:55:53.720
the Boltzmann equation is
for very weak scattering

00:55:53.720 --> 00:55:55.843
and for diluted limit.

00:55:55.843 --> 00:55:56.510
GANG CHEN: Right

00:55:56.510 --> 00:56:01.430
AUDIENCE: Here, why it can
apply to the shear stress law?

00:56:01.430 --> 00:56:04.400
GANG CHEN: I didn't
say applied to liquid.

00:56:04.400 --> 00:56:09.550
You're going to do-- this only
apply to gas, gas molecules.

